findings.recommendation:
Add `\\` (backslash) to the set of characters that must be escaped in MarkdownV2 mode, and ensure it is escaped FIRST (before other special chars) to avoid double-escaping. The corrected constant and method:

private static final String MARKDOWN_V2_SPECIALS = "_*[]()~`>#+-=|{}.!";

// In escapeMarkdownV2, prepend backslash handling:
private static String escapeMarkdownV2(String value) {
    StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(value.length() + 8);
    for (int i = 0; i < value.length(); i++) {
        char c = value.charAt(i);
        if (c == '\\' || MARKDOWN_V2_SPECIALS.indexOf(c) >= 0) {
            sb.append('\\');
        }
        sb.append(c);
    }
    return sb.toString();
}

The loop handles backslash by checking `c == '\\'` first (or simply include it in the SPECIALS string as the first character). The corresponding test case must be added: a value containing `\\` should render as `\\\\` (double-escaped).

Add double-quote escaping to `escapeHtml`:

case '"' -> sb.append("&quot;");

This prevents any subscriber value from breaking out of an HTML attribute context, regardless of how the author structures the template. The test `htmlModeEscapesSubstitutedValues` should be extended to verify that a value containing `"` renders as `&quot;`.
